{
  "meta": {
    "task_type": "distress_signal",
    "learner_language": "en",
    "learner_state": "procrastinator_days_left",
    "techniques": ["light_humour", "mnemonic"],
    "thinking_mode": "short",
    "tools_used": []
  },
  "dialogue": [
    {
      "role": "student",
      "content": "i know exams are coming but i just dont start. i sit down, open the book, then do something else. whats wrong with me"
    },
    {
      "role": "teacher",
      "private_reasoning": "Learner state is procrastinator_days_left, not distress_signal, so Rule 7 does not trigger. Respond to procrastination directly, no filler praise, use profile numbers only: 63 days, in 9 weeks, 3 hours per day, weak topics probability_counting and analytical_geometry. One light humour line. Two teacher turns total.",
      "content": "Nothing is wrong with you. Starting is the hard part; the book never attacks back.\n\nLook at the facts: your exams are in 9 weeks (63 days), and you have 3 hours a day. You do not need to feel like it. You need a first move so small it is embarrassing.\n\nTry this today: 10 minutes on Mathematics only, on one of your two weak areas — probability counting or analytical geometry. Then stop if you want. That is the whole task.",
      "tool_call": null
    },
    {
      "role": "student",
      "content": "ok i can do 10 minutes. but i forget what to do each time and then i just dont"
    },
    {
      "role": "teacher",
      "private_reasoning": "Learner accepts. Now give the mnemonic technique, built only from spec items (subjects and weak topics), plus light touch. No invented numbers, no exam predictions.",
      "content": "Then give your brain a hook so you never have to decide.\n\nSay it in order: **M-E-A**.\n**M** — Maths first, while your head is fresh, on probability counting or analytical geometry.\n**E** — English next.\n**A** — Afrikaans FAL last, when you are slower anyway.\n\nM-E-A. Like a meal: you eat in that order, no negotiating. Tonight you only owe the M, 10 minutes. Tell me tomorrow if you did it, and we will pick the next small step.
